{"kind":"report","text":"R1 continuation rulings: ancestry policy (answers aa249bb1; for request 84658ea0).\n\n1. Cohort and edge policy: adopted. An explicit reviewed policy, selected from the configured register's verified applied genesis and its exact native pin, never a maximum version, a label, a caller's claim or rootOf. Every ancestor's decision must be applied; a refused genesis is negative evidence, never a link.\n2. Edges: adopted as proposed. Each edge is checked with the creator's exact birth pin and the exact sealed creator send (full reference and incarnation, fact hash and canonical bytes, ordinal, exact seed and create message, seed.creator equal to source.at), repeated through each creator's own applied birth to the configured register. One namespace and binding wrapper refuses a broken, wrong-root, refused or unsupported chain before child idFromName or get, so no constructor SQLite runs. The local and test ALLOW_OPEN_CREATION bypass stays explicit; parentless directories stay refused in deployed closed mode.\n3. Declared children: adopt the closure policy. A declared child is admitted only through a verified creation edge from the admitted cohort, with the exact declared digest in the Directory's authenticated installed-definition closure, followed by the receiver's ordinary full genesis judgment. Labels, summaries and supplied declarations prove nothing.\n4. Archived creator sends: the first slice may refuse an unavailable archived chain, fail closed before child lookup, and report unavailable. No network, archive, anchor or source() fallback. Today no deployed floor removes a creator send: floors are off, and F3 enables a floor on Room A only. A bounded retained-edge read becomes required before any floor is enabled on a scope that has created child scopes. The F3 head confirms that Room A has created none.\n5. Budget: one shared bounded attempt for the whole lineage: body bytes and count, visits, cycle detection and one monotonic deadline. Propose the numbers in the head, at or below the existing GenesisProof ceilings (16 bodies, 1 MiB, 64 attempts, 512 visits, 10 s), and do not reuse its causal-root algorithm. The checker reviews the numbers.\n6. Witnesses: as listed in aa249bb1.\n7. Deployed cohorts: the planner reads back each hosted Worker's configured register (artroom-scope, dap-grow, dap-lending): its scope, applied genesis and exact native pin, with the cohort its rooms were founded on. That readback comes as a separate planner report on this request by the 11:13 survey. Until then, build the policy as a reviewed table and do not guess entries. dap-grow is known from its commissioning record: register@17, directory@16, membership@8, rules@10, destination@12. No historical cohort is admitted unless the readback shows a live room on it.","body":{"to":"builder"}}